Manual review corrections, all local LaTeX/typographic defects rather than
mathematical errors, except one probable spurious duplicate condition:

1. Exercise 1 ("Bounded by the curves y=3x,x=0, and y=3..."): unlike its
   four otherwise-identical siblings in the same list (all of which fuse
   all three bounding equations into one math span with \,\text{and}\,),
   this exercise split off the third equation into its own math span with
   plain ", and " between. Normalized to match the sibling items' own
   established convention in this file.

2. Exercise "y = sin x^2, x = 0, and x = sqrt(pi)": unparenthesized
   \sin x^{2} is ambiguous (sin(x^2) vs (sin x)^2) and inconsistent with
   every other trig argument in this file, which is parenthesized (e.g.
   \sin(\pi y), \cos(\pi x)). Added parentheses: \sin(x^{2}).

3. Exercise "y = (1+x^2)^3, ...": redundant outer braces around an
   already-parenthesized base, {(1+x^{2})}^{3}. Removed the redundant
   braces: (1+x^{2})^{3}.

4. Six exercises in the "use shells ... around the x-axis" list state
   the x-axis/y-axis as a bare, unmathified fourth boundary condition
   ("... and the x-axis"), while every other axis mention throughout this
   entire file (over twenty occurrences) uses $x\text{-axis}$/
   $y\text{-axis}$. Mathified these six to match this file's own
   overwhelming, consistent convention.

5. Exercise "x = cuberoot(27y) and x = 3y/4": the source is missing the
   \, before \text{and} that every sibling item in this file carries,
   so "27y" and "and" glue together with no separating space
   (matches a previously found recurring OpenStax MathML/converter
   spacing defect: an omitted <mspace> before an <mtext> token). Restored
   the missing \, .

6. Exercise "Consider the region enclosed by the graphs of y=f(x),
   y=1+f(x), x=0, y=0, and x=a>0...": this is the only region-description
   list in the whole corpus with five bounding conditions where every
   sibling-style exercise (including the very same "two curves and two
   vertical lines" pattern used throughout this file) has exactly four.
   Given y=f(x) and y=1+f(x) already fully bound the region vertically
   (they differ by the constant 1, so one is always above the other) and
   x=0, x=a>0 bound it horizontally, "y=0" fits no natural role here and
   makes the description an ill-defined five-condition list. Judged this
   to be a spurious duplicated boundary (a converter/source defect of the
   same shape as a previously found stray duplicate item) and removed it,
   restoring the standard four-condition form. Flagging this one as a
   judgment call in case the upstream source genuinely intends a fifth
   condition.

7. Final exercise: sentence punctuation trapped inside math mode,
   "$f^{-1}(y).)$" -- the closing paren belongs to the outer "(Hint: ...)"
   parenthetical in plain prose, not inside the math span. Corrected to
   "$f^{-1}(y)$.)".

8. Four "method of shells" application exercises (sphere, cone, ellipsoid,
   cylinder) have accessibility figure descriptions that mention the same
   $r$/$h$/$a$/$b$ variables used in the exercise's own math as bare,
   unmathified letters in plain prose ("radius r", "height h", "a the
   horizontal distance..."). Matching the corpus's own established
   precedent (vol3-4-5-the-chain-rule.tex, where equivalent plain-prose
   figure-description variable mentions were mathified to match the main
   text), mathified these to $r$/$h$/$a$/$b$. Left the "radius of the path
   between them is 2 units" donut description alone, since it names a
   number, not a bare variable letter.

